7592186496132 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "6c9253cc-52ac-5d1c-b3ee-ba5595fbaccc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496212 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496212 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496212 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496212 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496212 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496212 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496212 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496212 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186495898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496212 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f6021314-f875-5954-b86b-11e994619d15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495694 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496213 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186495895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496213 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496213 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496213 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186495895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496213 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f644e2e4-a4d8-546c-9e9b-b5558284b434" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496231 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496231 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105441" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496231 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496231 #edb/ref 1071 "A car dealership with $7$ sellers sells $50$ cars in a month. The owner uses the formula\n\n$$\nN=\\frac{c}{s}\n$$\n\nto determine, on average, the number of cars sold by each seller, where $c$ is the total of cars sold, and $s$ is the total number of sellers.\n\nFind an appropriate measurement unit for $N.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496231 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "177c4027-65c0-5159-869d-c7c5edbf2c0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496231 #edb/ref 1074 "We are solving for measurement units, so we can ignore the quantities $50$ and $7.$ They do not matter if we are just solving for units.\n\nSince $c$ represents $\\text{cars}$ and $s$ represents $\\text{sellers},$ the appropriate measurement unit for $N=\\frac{c}{s}$ is $\\frac{\\text{cars}}{\\text{seller}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496261 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496261 #edb/ref 1069 "q-46155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496261 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496261 #edb/ref 1071 "The work applied to an object is the result of multiplying the force by the distance. Given that the force is measured in Newtons $(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}),$ determine the unit of work in terms of kilograms, meters and seconds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496261 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e4a27b0-0664-5456-aded-ea139acd3d27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496261 #edb/ref 1074 "The force is measured in Newtons $(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}})$ and distance is measured in meters ($\\text{m}$). Since the work is the multiplication of force by distance, we must multiply force units by distance units. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{m}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThus, the unit of work is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496290 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496290 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105397" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496290 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496290 #edb/ref 1071 "The magnitude of a force $F$ applied to an object is related to the work $W$ and the distance $d$ by the formula $F=\\frac{W}{d}.$  Given that $W$ is measured in $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}$ and the distance is measured in meters, determine an appropriate measurement unit for the force." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496290 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d7c30aa-a3e9-5b45-be5b-cfccc139072e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496290 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the unit of the force, we substitute the given units of work and distance into the formula and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =\\frac{W}{d} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}})}{\\text{m}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\text{m}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{m}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot \\cancel{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of the force is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496319 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496110 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496319 #edb/ref 1069 "q-46150" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496319 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496319 #edb/ref 1071 "The formula that relates the work $W$ applied to an object with mass $m$, distance $d$ and time $t$ is $W=\\frac{m{d}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}.$ What are the units of work in terms of base units?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496319 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4eb324e-31ac-540b-94ba-f7c7a69f257d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496319 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the unit of work, we substitute the base units of mass, distance, and time into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\\frac{m{d}^{2}}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of work is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496329 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496329 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105382" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496329 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496329 #edb/ref 1071 "The gravitational force between two particles of mass ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ that are at a distance $r$ from each other is given by the formula ${F}_{\\text{G}}=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}},$ where $G$ is the gravitational constant measured in $\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{3}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,\\text{kg}},$ masses ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are measured in kilograms, and the distance $r$ is measured in meters. Find an appropriate measurement unit for the gravitational force." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496329 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eced41ae-3fab-5c67-a3bf-aedcd9f64978" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496329 #edb/ref 1074 "We can find the unit of gravitational force by substituting the given unit of the gravitational constant, mass, and distance into the formula and simplifying it.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{\\text{G}} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{3}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,\\text{kg}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{kg}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{3}\\,{\\text{kg}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{m}\\cdot \\cancel{{\\text{m}}^{2}}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\cancel{\\text{kg}}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}\\,\\cancel{\\text{kg}}\\,\\cancel{{\\text{m}}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of the gravitational force is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496331 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496331 #edb/ref 1069 "q-46081" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496331 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496331 #edb/ref 1071 "The work $W$ applied to a body is related to force $F$ and distance $d$ by the equation $W=F\\cdot d.$ If $F$ is measured in Newtons $(\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}})$ and $d$ is measured in meters, what is an appropriate measurement unit for work?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496331 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f927f6ff-ccc6-56f9-848c-de38a72fd309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496331 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the unit of work, we substitute the given units of force and distance into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =F\\cdot d \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\text{m} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the unit of work is $\\frac{\\text{kg}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496334 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496334 #edb/ref 1069 "q-46090" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496334 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496334 #edb/ref 1071 "A printer prints $1\\,250$ letters in $60$ minutes. To determine the number of letters that the printer prints on average, the owner uses the formula\n\n$$\nN=\\frac{l}{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $l$ is the number of letters printed and $t$ the time in minutes.\n\nIn words, what is the appropriate measurement unit for $N?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496334 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ff3d9c94-77b4-51bb-806f-e93681c391b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186496334 #edb/ref 1074 "We are solving for measurement units, so we can ignore the quantities $1\\,250$ and $60.$ They do not matter, if we are just solving for units.\n\nSince $l$ represents $\\text{letters}$ and $t$ represents $\\text{minute},$ the appropriate measurement unit for $N=\\frac{l}{t}$ is $\\frac{\\text{letters}}{\\text{minute}}.$ In words, this is 'letters per minute'." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
